 position with tempo. The white king having already moved (KWF1) means it's stuck in the center, making checks particularly effective.\n\n**Reason:**\nB: b4-d2 gives check, forcing White's king to move (likely Kd1-e1 or Kd1-c1). This gains time to either castle next move or continue attacking. It's the most forcing continuation that seizes the initiative and exploits White's exposed king position. Given that White has advanced pieces (queen on c2, bishop on c7), I need to create counterplay immediately rather than passively defending.\n\n**Answer:** B: b4-d2"}
